  "pr_title": "docs(docs): add a teammate quickstart and a data-flow diagram",
  "pr_description": "## Goal\n\nThe README lets a teammate who is not a developer install spsync, upgrade it, and understand how data moves. The teammate needs no Terminal and no other document.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe README now has user instructions only. The Overview section has a Mermaid diagram that shows SharePoint, the OneDrive sync folder, the Word app, the CLI, the note, the `.spsync/` store, the plugin, and the GitHub release. The Setup section uses BRAT and the downloaded CLI, and ends with the Check setup command. Setup states that spsync supports only Macs with Apple silicon. A new Upgrade section describes the BRAT update at startup and the BRAT command that updates at once. The Usage section lists the plugin commands. It tells teammates to turn on \"Send edits as tracked changes\" for documents that other people own. Automatic sync stays on by default.\n\nThe new `CONTRIBUTING.md` holds the developer instructions: the uv install, the plugin build, the `cliPath` override, the CLI reference with `--suggest` and `push --dry-run`, the backends, the configuration and environment variable table, the project structure, the git hooks, the tests, the Word fixture checks, the standalone build, and the release link.\n\n`CODING_STANDARDS.md` no longer requires the README to list every environment variable.\n\nThe guide link moves to #13, because #13 creates `docs/guide.md`.\n\n## Testing\n\n- `uv run ruff format --check`, `uv run ruff check`, `uv run ty check`, `uv run anti-slop-python src tests`, and `uv run pytest` pass. The change has no code.\n- The Mermaid diagram renders with `@mermaid-js/mermaid-cli`.\n- `rg` finds no em-dash and no curly quotation marks in the changed files.\n- Manual check: open the README on the GitHub branch page, and confirm that the diagram renders.\n\n## Issue\n\nCloses #12\n"
